From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] installd compiles everything when the profile cannot be read either

Patch 0006 asks: is there a reference profile with something in it? If not, the
compilation that was going to be profile-guided is done with "speed" instead,
because a profile-guided filter with no profile compiles nothing and an
application with no compiled code is unusable on this CPU.

That question has a second answer the check does not cover: there is a profile,
and dex2oat cannot read it. A profile is written by the merge that runs when the
device is idle on the charger, to the reference profile's own file, truncating
it first; a reboot or a power loss inside that window leaves a file that is not
empty and not a profile. dex2oat does not then fall back to compiling
everything -- it stops ("Failed to process profile file", dex2oat/dex2oat.cc,
after ProfileCompilationInfo::Load returns false) -- and installd reports the
dexopt as failed, which leaves the application with exactly the no-compiled-code
patch 0006 exists to prevent, by the other road.

So the compilation becomes an attempt that can be made twice: where the caller
set DEXOPT_SPEED_IF_NO_PROFILE and the profile-guided attempt failed, the second
one compiles with "speed" and no profile. The loop is around the whole body, not
around the dex2oat call, because each attempt opens its own output oat, output
vdex, application image and swap file, and the Dex2oatFileWrapper of a failed
attempt unlinks what it wrote when the iteration ends; the second attempt then
starts from nothing. (The input vdex is gone by then, since opening the output
vdex recreated the path it is read from, so the second attempt re-verifies the
dex file. That costs time on a boot that is already compiling, and it is what
compiling with no usable prior work means.)

The retry is not limited to the profile being the reason, because installd
cannot tell from the exit status which reason it was, and there is no failure of
a profile-guided dexopt whose better answer here is to leave the application
without compiled code. It is limited to callers that asked for the fallback --
the recompilation after a system update -- and to one retry, so a dex2oat that
fails for a reason of its own fails twice at most.

